WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.140
Speaker 1: All right, let's get into audience
questions for the week, the first one is related

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:09.089
to the Cuban protests and and a situation
that we've been talking about there for about

00:00:09.089 --> 00:00:10.230
ten days now.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:14.570
David, why do some protests get so much more
media attention than others?

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:18.551
For example, there are currently protests
happening in Guatemala and South Africa, and

00:00:18.551 --> 00:00:22.210
yet we hear nothing about them in American
media, but we hear a lot about Cuba.

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:24.870
Why is this so the answer?

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:26.330
There's two different answers.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:33.079
First, there's a sort of a commercialization
of media answer and then there's a political

00:00:33.079 --> 00:00:34.079
answer.

00:00:34.079 --> 00:00:36.399
And in the case of Cuba, it's actually a little
bit of both.

00:00:36.399 --> 00:00:40.739
But it's absolutely true in the sense that
first, let's figure out if the situations

00:00:40.739 --> 00:00:46.270
are different in terms of their level of notoriety
in Cuba.

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:52.499
We've seen significant protests as well as
serious crackdowns, many videos getting out

00:00:52.499 --> 00:00:57.789
of Cuban police, federal, local police, you
know, variety of different levels of law enforcement

00:00:57.789 --> 00:01:04.120
in Cuba, just grabbing people, holding signs
off the street and a significant and serious

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:05.120
crackdown.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:10.450
The protests have been related to mishandling
of covid out of control, inflation, a collapsing

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:14.380
economy, lack of resources, lack of food,
all sorts of different things.

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:16.520
Similarly, in South Africa.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:22.000
There have been at least 32 people killed
in clashes between police and protesters.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:28.280
The president of South Africa ordered soldiers
to the country's two most densely populated

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:33.080
provinces because crowds were ransacking retail
stores.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:39.160
And the protests were in response to the jailing
of the former president, Jacob Zuma.

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:42.770
And there's it's been significantly chaotic
in South Africa.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:47.860
Similarly, in Guatemala, there have been demands
for the resignation of the president over

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:52.210
his mishandling of the covid pandemic, a shortage
of vaccines, et cetera.

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:55.730
There were protests there in Guatemala City,
as well as in other regions denouncing the

00:01:55.730 --> 00:02:03.370
president's handling of the situation, demands
for the president giammetti to step down.

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:05.409
And cases are skyrocketing.

00:02:05.409 --> 00:02:07.710
Hospitals are about to collapse.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:11.640
Very low vaccination rate, significant chaos
in Guatemala.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.740
But yet we barely hear about those in American
media, but we hear about Cuba.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:18.300
So what is the explanation?

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.930
There's really two one up.

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:27.000
The history of U.S. Cuban relations is a big
part of it.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:33.770
Cuban circumstances in Cuba relate to so many
American political stories, including this

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:38.620
battle over socialism and communism, that
the right has been fighting against communists

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.390
like Joe Biden.

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:41.750
I mean, that's ridiculous, right?

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:46.360
But it's very useful for the right to talk
about Cuba and anything bad that happens in

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:47.850
Cuba.

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:53.470
There's also a much more important from a
political standpoint, Cuban-American population

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:57.180
in terms of its impact on the results in Florida.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:01.390
And so politically, it's a much more useful
population.

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:10.290
You know, most non Guatemalans are super concerned
about the Guatemalan American vote as a voting

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:13.430
bloc in the United States for them.

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:16.020
And this applies to Argentinean Americans
like me.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:17.020
And.

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:18.020
Right.

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:19.470
I mean, there's there's just less political
utility.

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:25.030
Mexico is certainly an interesting one because
of political reasons, and Cuba is as well.

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:32.709
Similarly, South African Americans not like
Cuban Americans in terms of a group that a

00:03:32.709 --> 00:03:33.989
state can hinge on.

00:03:33.989 --> 00:03:39.540
So in particular, in that context, it's logical
that we're hearing more about Cuba than we

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:42.210
are about Guatemala and South Africa.

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:47.640
And then there's just like the straight up
commercial narrative story, which narrative

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:55.460
is going to make news viewers tune in the
longest and either happy or angry at someone

00:03:55.460 --> 00:04:01.110
so that they continue to share articles and
sort of engage because that drives ratings

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:03.300
and ad revenue.

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:08.820
Because of the first piece that I mentioned,
there's not as much need or interest in appealing

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:12.310
to Guatemalan Americans or South African Americans.

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:14.050
It's not going to drive ratings.

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:16.720
It's not going to drive news revenue.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:19.440
Talking about Cuba, very, very much does.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.000
And it's a similar question actually to why
do some shootings get more attention than

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:24.000
others?

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:31.330
The right loves to say only shootings of minorities
by white people get attention because that

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:33.272
serves the left.

00:04:33.272 --> 00:04:38.710
The left often points out that the right in
general doesn't want to talk about shootings

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:40.900
unless the perpetrator is nonwhite.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:44.380
And it's like very, very clean along racial
lines.

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:47.510
Both stories are the reality is there's much
more to it.

00:04:47.510 --> 00:04:48.620
There's power dynamics.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:54.090
There's what was it an officer involved shooting
or was this civilians?

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:58.620
But there's also I mean, when you look at
how many mass shootings take place every day,

00:04:58.620 --> 00:04:59.840
there's clearly a selection.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:05.880
Bias that's taking place and it's much similarly
oriented along the same lines to two things

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:10.310
one, what will generate interest from the
standpoint of being a news story?

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:15.750
And number two, which shootings are of political
relevance to either or both sides?

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:20.510
And it's a very similar thing with which with
the story of which protests get attention.

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:24.139
There's something I'm missing in this analysis
is there's more to it.

00:05:24.139 --> 00:05:26.850
Guatamala, South Africa, Cuba, certainly.

00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:27.850
Let me know.

00:05:27.850 --> 00:06:24.419
I would love to 

00:06:24.419 --> 00:06:31.680
hear it.

